# noergler team self-service, IntelliJ HTTP client. Pick an environment
# (dev / prod): copy http-client.env.json.example to http-client.env.json (gitignored), fill in host, team, secret and
# the tokens there; do not commit them.
#   {{secret}}      the team's webhook secret (from the noergler admin): authenticates every call
#   {{bot_user}}    your bot account's Bitbucket username: the team then @mentions it, not the shared bot
#   {{bot_token}}   that bot account's HTTP access token (write on your repos, no admin needed)
#   {{jira_token}}  a Jira personal access token for the ticket check
# Tokens are checked against Bitbucket / Jira before they are stored, stored encrypted, and never returned.

### Your own bot. Afterwards run grant-bot (2-claim.http) so the new bot can write;
### the answer's unreachable_claims lists what it cannot read yet.
PUT {{host}}/teams/{{team}}/credentials
Authorization: Bearer {{secret}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bitbucket": {"username": "{{bot_user}}", "token": "{{bot_token}}"}
}

### Your own Jira token
PUT {{host}}/teams/{{team}}/credentials
Authorization: Bearer {{secret}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "jira": {"token": "{{jira_token}}"}
}

### Which accounts the team uses, and whether they work
GET {{host}}/teams/{{team}}
Authorization: Bearer {{secret}}

### Back to the shared bot (and the shared @mention)
DELETE {{host}}/teams/{{team}}/credentials/bitbucket
Authorization: Bearer {{secret}}

### Back to the shared Jira user
DELETE {{host}}/teams/{{team}}/credentials/jira
Authorization: Bearer {{secret}}
